{"kind":"request","text":"State the job gitseq does, lead with the solo-developer-in-an-organization route, and give the prompt that runs it. Hugh's judgement, 2026-08-10, in his words: I want to use multiple local agents with individual skills and capabilities, coordinating around a development project, and gitseq is a do-it-yourself way to get there. The documentation implies that job rather than stating it. README.md leads with a different and also true proposition, document currency, arguing that every document is a cache nobody invalidates; agents appear incidentally, as one item in a list at line 76. Say the job near the top in the reader's words, and let the cache argument follow as the reason the record must be ordered and signed rather than a wiki. Second, the easiest onboarding is a developer running gs init on a local copy of a repository they already work in, with their own agents, and no expectation that the organization stands up a central sequencer; the shared-genesis model is functionally dependent on containerization, which is not planned yet. Every entry path today starts from an empty directory, docs/how-to/end-to-end.md opens with mktemp -d, and nothing covers adopting gitseq alone inside an organization that has adopted nothing, which reads as a precondition that the organization must agree first. Third, and missing entirely: the prompt. Installing the workroom does not make agents coordinate; giving each agent a standing instruction does. Hugh's, used in practice and to be quoted verbatim: Using the gitseq mcp server, check for work items and prioritize appropriately to keep work progressing. Dispatch tasks to max 3 subagents. Continue checking every 10 minutes indefinitely. It is short, it is runtime-neutral across codex, claude and others, and it is the difference between a repository with a log in it and a project that moves. This request replaces e6807072, which lacked it.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced after the ownership-descent identit